<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utu-hengal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2120 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Utu-hegal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Utu-hengal was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> in the late 22nd century BCE who led the Sumerian cities against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a>. His victory over the Gutian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirigan">Tirigan</a> ended Gutian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, and he took the title 'king of the four quarters'. His governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, possibly his brother, succeeded him and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utu-hengal.jpg" alt="Utu-hengal" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred C. Weatherstone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Utu-Khegal,_Prince_of_the_Summerian_city_of_Erech,_imploring_victory_against_the_Gutian_king_Tirikan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
